<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantin Carathéodory</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Constantin Caratheodory</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantin Carathéodory (1873–1950) was a Greek mathematician who spent most of his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He made major contributions to the calculus of variations, measure theory and the theory of functions, and gave an axiomatic formulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermodynamics">thermodynamics</a>. In 1920 the Greek government invited him to organise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-university-of-smyrna">Ionian University of Smyrna</a>, which was never able to open; he later taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantin-caratheodory.jpg" alt="Constantin Carathéodory" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author; derivative work by Pbrks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caratheodory_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constantin Carathéodory was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 13 September 1873 to Greek parents and grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. His father Stephanos, a lawyer, was the Ottoman ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, St Petersburg and Berlin, and his mother Despina came from the island of Chios; she died in 1879. He twice won a prize as the best mathematics student in Belgium at the Athénée Royal d'Ixelles, and from 1891 trained as a military engineer at the Belgian Royal Military Academy.</p><p>When war broke out between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1897 he sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, while his father served the Ottoman government. He took a post in the British colonial service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, working on the Assiut dam until April 1900 and teaching himself mathematics during the flood stoppages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>, where he took his doctorate in 1904 and his habilitation in 1905. He became professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hannover</a> in 1909 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a> in 1910, succeeded Felix Klein at Göttingen in 1913 and moved to Berlin in 1919. At the invitation of Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> he submitted a plan on 20 October 1919 for a university at Smyrna, and in 1920 he became its dean, touring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to buy books and equipment.</p><p>It never admitted students because the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> ended in the Great Fire of Smyrna. A journalist rowed him to the battleship Naxos, and he brought part of the library to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where he taught until 1924. He then held the chair at the Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> until his retirement in 1938 and worked at the Bavarian Academy of Sciences until his death on 2 February 1950. In 1936 he handed out the first Fields Medals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His dissertation extended solutions in the calculus of variations to discontinuous cases, and his method of equivalent variational problems became known as the royal road to the subject. The Carathéodory extension theorem is fundamental to modern measure theory. In complex analysis he proved that conformal mappings extend to the boundary of Jordan domains and originated the theory of prime ends.</p><p>In 1909 he formulated the second law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermodynamics">thermodynamics</a> axiomatically, without Carnot engines, and coined the term adiabatic accessibility; Max Born praised the work, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a> doubted it was a fundamental formulation. In 1926 he proved that no system of lenses and mirrors can avoid aberration, except plane mirrors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantin Carathéodory” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Greek university, the Ionian University, was planned under the mathematician <b>Constantin Caratheodory</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/smyrna-zone">Smyrna Zone</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constantin Carathéodory"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
